\begin_layout Chapter
Context Ensemble Local Learning (CELL)
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "chap:Context-Ensemble-Local"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The literature review presented in previous chapter highlighted a need for reliable learning architectures able to fit into a Model Predictive Control (MPC) framework and to adapt to complex non-stationary environments without forgetting previously acquired knowledge.
 Traditional approaches using fixed global models often fail to adapt when faced with the shifting behavior of human agents or changing environmental conditions.
 Local online learning approaches have shown promise in meeting these requirements.
 In this chapter,
 we specifically explore learning approaches based on multi-agent systems based on local experts self-organizing from social feedbacks.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Building upon the Self-Adaptive Context Learning (SACL) paradigm 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "boesSelfAdaptiveContextLearning2015"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 the Context Ensemble Local Learning (CELL) formalism is introduced.
 While SACL separates the system into two decoupled layers,
 one for adaptation and the other one for exploitation,
 CELL moves toward a more unified architecture.
 The multi-agent ensemble serves as the model of the dynamics itself aiming at being integrated direcly into the control loop.
 it allows the social rules governing the self-organization of agents to manage the learning task while providing the MPC with a continuous locally accurate representation of the environment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
One of the advantages of self-organizing multi-agent systems lies in the transparency of the internal operations and the interactions between agents.
 This transparency allows for the extraction and analysis of introspective properties about the system.
 In this work,
 introspection can be defined as
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
Introspection designates the properties that emerges naturally from the self-organizing mechanisms of the system,
 such as the geometric structure and density of agents within the input space.
 These emergent properties provide a window into the system's inner workings,
 allowing to gather information about the learning process or the reliability of a prediction.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Within CELL,
 an iterative process has been unfolded through two specific instantiations moving from discrete to continuous spatial representations.
 The first instantiations,
 Orthotope-based CELL (oCELL),
 where agents are spatialized using orthotopes in the input space.
 This instantiation allowed to study the capabilities of this type of learning system in terms of prediction accuracy in supervised learning as well as the link between the spatial organization of agents and the traditional machine learning notions of explainability and interpretability.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Building on oCELL,
 to address the limitations of discrete spatialization,
 Kernel-based CELL (kCELL) is introduced.
 This second instantiation of CELL leverages radial basis functions to create smoother continuous representations of the learned dynamics.
 Furthermore,
 kCELL introduces more interactive social rules where agents adapt more reactively relative to their neighbors or a collective baseline.
 This evolution leads to more faithful data representation and enhanced adaptation capabilities that are demonstrated through experiments on both stationary and non-stationary learning tasks.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The contributions of this chaper are threefold:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
We introduce the CELL formalism to frame the design of multi-agent systems based on local expert agents for supervised regression tasks within a control context.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Through the oCELL instantiation of CELL,
 we demonstrate the capabilities of such a system to be competitive in terms of predictive accuracy compared to state-of-the-art ensemble learning algorithms on a 2D supervised regression task.
 We also show that socially emergent properties can be linked to the notions of explainability and interpretability in machine learning.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Through the kCELL instantiation of CELL,
 we demonstrate that a system derived from CELL can be able to compete with state-of-the-art online learning algorithms in terms of predictive accuracy on a stationary learning task and to adapt its internal representations in response to changes in dynamics in a non-stationary environment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Separator plain
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The remainder of this chapter is organized to reflect the process that lead to a usable system within MPC context.
 First,
