\begin_layout Subsection
From SACL to Coupled Learning and Control
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:From-SACL-to-Coupled-Learning-and-Control"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The inspiration for the CELL formalism lies in the Self-Adaptive Context Learning (SACL) paradigm 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "boesSelfAdaptiveContextLearning2015"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 SACL was proposed as a design framework for multi-agent systems to solve complex,
 real-world problems by learning to map environmental situations,
 referred to as contexts,
 to the most effective actions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In SACL,
 the controller is defined through two primary entities:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
The Adaptation Mechanism
\series default
:
 Its goal is to build,
 maintain and provide reliable knowledge about the current context and potential actions.
 It correlates the activity of the controller to observations about the environment to identify contextual boundaries and model the local dynamics.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
The Exploitation Mechanism
\series default
:
 This is the entity responsible for applying actions on the dynamical system to be controlled.
 it uses the knowledge provided by the Adaptation Mechanism,
 alongside domain-specific constraints to decide which control action to apply.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Separator plain
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The interactions between these layers is governed by a continuous information loop (c.f.
 Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:SACL"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

).
 The Adaptation Mechanism perceives the environment and suggests possible actions and the Exploitation Mechanism chooses an action to perform.
 It then emits a feedback that is send to the Adaptation Mechanism to refine the context-action mapping.
 This decoupling between the modeling and control layers offers modularity but it treats the learning system as an external adviser to the controller.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Float figure
placement document
alignment document
wide false
sideways false
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename assets/SACL.pdf
	lyxscale 50
	width 70col%

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Caption Standard

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The Self-Adaptive Context Learning (SACL) Paradigm
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "fig:SACL"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The intelligence of the Adaptation Mechanism resides in an ensemble of 
\emph on
Context
\emph default
 agents.
 These agents are the core adaptive software entities that implement the social learning component of the system.
 Each 
\emph on
Context
\emph default
 agent is defined by a tripartite structure representing a specific tile of knowledge:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Context
\series default
:
 This is a set of validity ranges (one for each input feature).
 An agent is considered valid only if the current environmental data falls within all its specified ranges.
 It represents
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Action
\series default
:
 It corresponds to a control command to be applied to the controlled system that is proposed by the agent (e.g.
 torque set point or 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

go forward
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 command).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Appreciation
\series default
:
 It corresponds to a forecast of the action's effect on the controlled system.
 It is often expressed as a confidence or utility value.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In SACL,
 the valid agents emit propositions of actions that are forwarded to the Exploitation Mechanism that itself gives feedback to the corresponding agents to allow them to improve.
 This structure allows for dynamic cooperative self-organization through the detection and solving of issues,
 called Non-Cooperative Situations (NCS),
 in the self-organization process that break the nominal cooperative behavior of agents.
 The solving procedures defined for each NCS are the learning rules of the ensemble of 
\emph on
Context
\emph default
 agents.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As explored in Chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "chap:State-of-the-Art"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 the requirements of safety-critical control in non-stationary environments suggest a potential for integrating a model based on an ensemble of local expert agents in to a traditional MPC architecture.
 However,
 this integration necessitates a pivot from the SACL paradigm.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
SACL serves as a complete framework for behavioral control that includes environment modeling components.
 It is essentially a reinforcement learning approach where agents seek to suggest optimal actions.
 In contrast,
 CELL is designed as a supervised learning formalism for modeling system dynamics.
 This transition results in three gaps between the original SACL paradigm and the CELL formalism.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In SACL,
 the 
\emph on
Context
\emph default
 agent suggest actions and evaluate the action's utility.
 In CELL,
 the goal is not to determine the best action,
 but to provide a faithful approximation of the function 
\begin_inset Formula $f:\mathbb{R}^{n}\mapsto\mathbb{R}^{m}$
\end_inset

 underlying the data 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y=f\left(x\right)
\]

\end_inset

with 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in\mathbb{R}^{n}$
\end_inset

 a vector of input features and 
\begin_inset Formula $y\in\mathbb{R}^{m}$
\end_inset

 a vector of targets representing the true values.
 In the context of this thesis,
 the purpose of CELL is to approximate the transition function of the controlled dynamical system.
 In this setting,
 the input vector 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in\mathbb{R}^{n}$
\end_inset

 typically represents the state-action pair 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(s_{t},u_{t}\right)$
\end_inset

 at timestep 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

,
 while the target 
\begin_inset Formula $y\in\mathbb{R}^{m}$
\end_inset

 represents the state transition (either the next state 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{t+1}$
\end_inset

 or the state difference 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta_{t}=s_{t+1}-s_{t}$
\end_inset

) such as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
s_{t+1}=f\left(s_{t},u_{t}\right)
\]

\end_inset

Consequently,
 instead of corresponding to a local control policy,
 the agents in CELL correspond to local approximation of the transition function,
 allowing the ensemble to act as a global dynamics model of the controlled system.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Moreover,
 SACL treats the multi-agent system as an external advisor that interacts with an exploitation layer via a proposal-and-feedback loop.
 In contrast,
 the proposed architecture follows a more tightly coupled approach using the adaptive and self-organizing strengths of multi-agent systems with local experts for the modeling task and embeds this model within a traditional optimization-based control framework (MPC) (c.f.
 Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:CELL"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

).
 In this configuration,
 the information-sharing step is replaced by a direct query.
 The MPC optimizer does not wait for propositions but instead queries the agent ensemble at high frequency to predict future states or to extract the gradient necessary for the optimization of a control trajectory.
 This tight coupling ensures that the system benefits from the adaptability of multi-agent learning with an ensemble of local expert agents while maintaining the robustness brought by optimization-based control within MPC.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Finally,
 in the SACL paradigm,
 the behavior of the ensemble is primarily governed by the detection and resolution of NCS,
 focusing on the social cooperative harmony to maximize the utility of agents.
 While CELL retains the governance by social rules,
 it expands the social dynamics to include competition alongside cooperation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The next section details the Local Expert Agent formalism within CELL,
 defining their core components.
\end_layout

