\begin_layout Subsection
Local Expert Agents
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Local-Expert-Agents"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The previous section established the lineage of CELL from the SACL paradigm and identified the necessary shifts for an integration in a control loop within the MPC framework.
 In this section,
 the notion of Local Expert Agent within CELL is defined.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In CELL,
 the global task of modeling complex,
 non-linear dynamics from a stream of data is decomposed into a population of autonomous agents.
 Each agent 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{i}$
\end_inset

 acts as a local expert for a specific area of expertise in the input space.
 An agent is defined by a triplet such as 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathcal{A}_{i}=\left\{ \phi_{i},f_{i},\mathcal{M}_{i}\right\} 
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{i}:\mathbb{R}^{n}\mapsto\left[0,1\right]$
\end_inset

 is the activation function,
 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{i}:\mathbb{R}^{n}\mapsto\mathbb{R}^{m}$
\end_inset

 the internal local model and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{M}_{i}$
\end_inset

 represents the internal state of the agent.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 
\series bold
activation function
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{i}$
\end_inset

 represents the area of expertise of the agent in the input space.
 It defines the spatial boundaries in which the agent is considered as an expert,
 i.e as being relevant to predict.
 For a given input vector 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in\mathbb{R}^{n}$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{i}\left(x\right)\in\left[0,1\right]$
\end_inset

 returns an activation value.
 This value is used by the spatial gating mechanism to select the most relevant agents to predict for 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 (the notion of gating mechanism is detailed in Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Operational-Pipelines"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

).
 An activation value of 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{i}\left(x\right)=1$
\end_inset

 implies that the agent is an expert to predict for 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

,
 it means that 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is In-Distribution (ID) for agent 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{i}$
\end_inset

,
 and conversely,
 an activation value of 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{i}\left(x\right)=0$
\end_inset

 implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is outside the area of expertise of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{i}$
\end_inset

,
 it means that 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is Out-of-Distribution (OoD).
 The geometry of this function can be anything and can be adapted to the problem encountered and to specific requirements related to computation time (e.g.
 computing RBF kernels values in 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is more computationally heavy than computing intersection between 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 and a set of orthotopes) or dimensionality of the inputs.
 Ultimately,
 the activation function answers to the following question:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align center
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset


\emph on
When should 
\emph default

\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{i}$
\end_inset

 
\emph on
predict ?
\emph default

\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The local 
\series bold
internal model
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{i}$
\end_inset

 contains the predictive expertise of the agent,
 which is relevant locally within the area of expertise defined by the activation function 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Each agent 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{i}$
\end_inset

 maintains its own set of parameters 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{i}$
\end_inset

 to map input values 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 to predictions values
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{y_{i}}=f_{i}\left(x,\theta_{i}\right)
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{y_{i}}$
\end_inset

 is the prediction of agent 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{i}$
\end_inset

 for input 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
 This formalism is model-agnostic,
 meaning that virtually any class of learning model could be used as internal model for the agents.
 Ultimately,
 the internal model answers to the following question:
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align center
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset


\emph on
What should 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{i}$
\end_inset

 predict?
\emph default

\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 
\series bold
internal state
\series default
 (
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{M}_{i}$
\end_inset

) encapsulates metadata about the agent (e.g.
 local performance metrics,
 maturity,
 age or the number of points seen),
 which may also include a memory of the last points encountered.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Through this tripartite structure,
 CELL creates a modular learning system.
 The behavior of the global model is a result of the collective output of the local expert agent that compose it.
 The health and knowledge of the system can be directly gathered by inspecting the internal states 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{M}_{i}$
\end_inset

 and the spatial distribution of agents across the input space through the parameters of the activation functions 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In addition to these fundamental components,
 agents are associated with operations that allow them to adapt individually and locally.
 Two types of operations must be defined in the context of CELL to enable local self-organization:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Shape Update
\series default
:
 It corresponds to updating the parameters of the activation function 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{i}$
\end_inset

,
 effectively changing the shape of the area of expertise of agent
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Depending on the social feedbacks received by agent 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{i}$
\end_inset

 for an input 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

,
 the latter can realize either that his area of expertise is well positioned within the input space,
 or that it is not.
 For example,
 if 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{i}$
\end_inset

 receives a bad feedback for its prediction on 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

,
 it might want to shrink its area of expertise to not include 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 anymore,
 and conversely if it receives a good feedback for its prediction on 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

,
 it might want to expand its area of expertise to include 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Model Update
\series default
:
 It corresponds to updating the parameters 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{i}$
\end_inset

 of the internal model 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{i}$
\end_inset

 to include a point 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
 For example,
 it could consist in a gradient descent step.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The next section details the inference and learning pipelines that allow respectively for the self-organization of agents and to obtain the predictions of the global model.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Operational Pipelines
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Operational-Pipelines"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
After formalizing in detail the main components that compose the agents in previous section,
 this section presents the learning loop structure of a CELL system and how the predictions of the global system are obtained from the set of local expert agents.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A CELL system operates through 2 pipelines:
 the 
\series bold
inference pipeline
\series default
,
 which produces a global prediction from the ensemble of agents,
 and the 
\series bold
learning pipeline
\series default
,
 which governs the social self-organization of agents.
 Both pipelines rely on a spatial gating mechanism to manage the locallity in the system.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For a given input 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 the spatial gating mechanism select the a subset of relevant local expert agents to predict among the ensemble of agents.
 This spatial gating mechanism guarantees spatial isolation of parameters inside the learning system.
 For any input,
 the spatial gating mechanism outputs two subsets of agents:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Activated Agents
\series default
 (
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{\text{activated}}$
\end_inset

):
 Agents for which 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 falls within their area of expertise (i.e 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is ID for activated agents and OoD for non activated agents).
 These agents are the most relevant agents to contribute to the final prediction.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Neighbor Agents
\series default
 (
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{\text{neighbors}}$
\end_inset

):
 The set of neighbor agents is a superset of activated agents.
 In addition to including activated agents,
 it includes agents for which 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is not yet ID but is close to their spatial boundaries.
 These agents represent potential improvement for the system as they are candidate for updating the shape of their area fo expertise to include 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 and become expert on it.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Separator plain
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To obtain the global prediction of the ensemble of local expert agents,
 the 
\series bold
inference pipeline 
\series default
(illustrated in Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:CELL-Inference-Pipeline"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) aims,
 from an input 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

,
 to select the most relevant agents and aggregate their local predictions.
 The inference process follows four distinct steps:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Distance Evaluation
\series default
:
 The degree of expertise on 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 of each individual agent is computed using their activation function 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{i}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Expert Selection
\series default
 
\series bold
(Gating)
\series default
:
 The spatial gating mechanism filters the ensemble of selected agents to identify the most relevant agents to predict for 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 outputing two subsets of agents:
 the activated agents 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{\text{activated}}$
\end_inset

 and the neighbor agents 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{\text{neighbors}}$
\end_inset

.
 For example,
 the activated and neighbor agents can be filtered based on a threshold on the activation value such 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{i}\left(x\right)\geq\tau_{\text{activation}}$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{i}\left(x\right)\geq\tau_{\text{neighbor}}$
\end_inset

(with 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau_{\text{activation}}\in\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau_{\text{neighbor}}\in\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 two real scalar thresholds).
 The whole subset of selected agents 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{\text{selected}}$
\end_inset

 by the spatial gating mechanism is defined such as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathcal{D}_{\text{selected}}=\mathcal{D}_{\text{activated}}\cup\mathcal{D}_{\text{neighbors}}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Local Prediction
\series default
:
 Each selected agent (neighbor or activated depending on the prediction policy defined by the designer of the system) produces a prediction proposal based on its internal model:
 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{y}_{i}=f_{i}\left(x,\theta_{i}\right)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Aggregation
\series default
:
 The individual prediction of the most relevant experts are combined using an aggregation function (e.g.
 weighted average).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Separator plain
\end_inset


\end_layout

