\begin_layout Standard
The 
\series bold
learning process
\series default
 of a specific CELL instantiation (such as oCELL or kCELL that will be described in subsequent sections of this chapter) can be fully characterized by constructing a rule table.
 This table acts as the behavioral blueprint for the ensemble of agents,
 mapping the current state of the population and the received feedback to a set of discrete actions.
 By using this structured approach,
 the designer can precisely define the social dynamics,
 such as competition for expertise or cooperative space-filling,
 without relying on global loss functions.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This rule table is defined by four primary columns:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Social Context
\series default
:
 The situational state of the ensemble relative to the current input.
 For exeample this context can be defined by the number of activated agents (
\begin_inset Formula $N_{\text{activated}}$
\end_inset

) or neighbor agents (
\begin_inset Formula $N_{\text{neighbors}}$
\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Condition (Trigger)
\series default
:
 The specific feedback value or internal state threshold (e.g.,
 error levels) that activate the rule.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Targeted Subset
\series default
:
 The group of agents subject to the rule (e.g.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{\text{activated}}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{\text{neighbors}}$
\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Action
\series default
:
 It corresponds to the action to be performed by the agents in the targeted subset or to the meta-action to apply.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The use of a Social Context ensures that the learning process is aware of the local social dynamics.
 The same error signal might lead to different actions depending on whether and agent is part of a group of experts (
\begin_inset Formula $N_{\text{activated}}\geq1$
\end_inset

),
 acting alone (
\begin_inset Formula $N_{\text{activated}}=1$
\end_inset

) or if the input 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is within a void (
\begin_inset Formula $N_{\text{neighbors}}=0$
\end_inset

).
 An example of a conceptual rule table using these social indicators is provided in Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:Conceptual-Learning-Rules"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

.
 This formalization ensures that the learning process remains decentralized and modular.
 
\end_layout

