\begin_layout Subsection
Discussion on Theoretical Properties
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Discussion-on-Theoretical-Properties"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The previous sections established the lineage of the CELL formalism,
 formalized its components and detailed the operational pipelines that govern the ensemble of local expert agents.
 This section discusses the theoretical properties of CELL systems,
 highlighting how it addresses the challenges of online learning in non-stationary environments and its suitability for integration within a control framework.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A primary challenge in online learning for dynamical system is 
\series bold
catastrophic forgetting
\series default
,
 where new information overwrites previously learned knowledge.
 CELL inherently mitigates this through its spatial gating mechanism,
 which ensures spatial isolation of parameters.
 Since learning rules and parameter updates are restricted to the subsets 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{\text{activated}}$
\end_inset

 and ,
 data from one region of the input space cannot easily corrupt the expertise of agents in non-related regions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
While traditional machine learning often relies on top-down global optimization,
 CELL is inherently multi-agent and adopts a bottom-up design based on social learning.
 In the context of CELL,
 the global dynamics model is not a predefined static structure but an emergent property of the self-organizing ensemble of local experts.
 Each agent operates with only partial knowledge of the global problem,
 perceiving its local social context,
 deciding on a rule to trigger based on its own feedback and acting to update its internal components.
 This decentralized decision-making process brings structural adaptability.
 It allows the model to modify its own internal structure through agent creation or destruction without th ecomputational burden or re-optimizing a global topology.
 By shifting the complexity from the model's architecture to the social dynamics between agents,
 the system can autonomously 
\series bold
expand its capacity in response to non-stationary dynamics
\series default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Furthermore,
 the formalism is 
\series bold
model-agnostic
\series default
,
 meaning that virtually any class of learning model could be used as the internal prediction model of the agents.
 This modularity favors a high level of transparency in the inner workings of the learning system.
 In the remainder of this thesis,
 simple,
 low-complexity models that can converge quickly such as linear regression are preferred because high-capacity models could be counterproductive,
 not receiving enough local data to converge,
 thereby hurting global performance.
 Using fast-converging model classes ensures better online learning performance and maintains the white-box nature of the inner workings,
 which represents a significant advantage over global black box models for safety-critical applications.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Finally,
 the integration of CELL within an optimization-based control framework like MPC necessitates some properties like 
\series bold
differentiability and fast inference
\series default
 (both of which are discussed in detail in Chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "chap:Solving-Control-Tasks"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

).
 While the agents provide local expertise,
 the aggregation function used to build the global prediction should ensure that the transitions between the areas of expertise of different agents is smooth.
 If the internal models,
 the activation functions and the aggregation function are all differentiable,
 the corresponding CELL instantiation produces differentiable predictions.
 This allows for the extraction of the necessary gradients 
\begin_inset Formula $\nabla_{x}f$
\end_inset

 (i.e the Jacobians of the dynamics with respect to state and action) for predicting future states or optimizing control trajectories within an MPC framework with gradient-based solvers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The real-time constraints of control loops require the model to perform high-frequency inference.
 In CELL instantiations,
 fast inference is facilitated by the use of low-complexity internal models and by the spatial gating mechanism that selects only a subset of relevant agents for prediction.
 However,
 maintaining a fast inference speed becomes challenging as the population grows,
 because evaluating the activation value of a very large number of agent for a given input 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 can rapidly represent a computational bottleneck.
 Consequently,
 achieving efficient scaling requires a carefully designed spatialization layer to minimize the cost of distance evaluations and ensure that the spatial gating process remains compatible with the timing requirements of the control task to solve.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Note Note
status collapsed

