\begin_layout Section
oCELL:
 CELL with Orthotope Spatialization
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:oCELL"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The CELL formalism described in previous section lays the theoretical ground for designing learning systems based on multi-agent ensemble of local expert agents.
 It establishes the operational logic of the ensemble of expert agents while remaining agnostic to the specific geometry of the expertise areas,
 to the internal model classes and to the social feedback used.
 Consequently,
 to transform this theoretical framework into a functional algorithm,
 one must defined the concrete form of the activation function 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{i}$
\end_inset

 and the specific set of social rules governing the population.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The first instantiation of CELL,
 oCELL (Orthotope-based CELL),
 leverages axis-aligned orthotopes to define agent boundaries.
 This choice builds on previous works on this family of multi-agent systems 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "dato2021apprentissage,fourez2024analyzing"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 This choice prioritize computational efficiency and geometric interpretability providing a baseline for evaluating the emergent modeling capabilities of the CELL framework in low dimensional input spaces.
 In this section,
 oCELL serves as a primary mean for demonstrating how emergent introspective properties of this ensemble of local expert agents provide useful insights about the learning process.
 This introspective properties are shown to be related to the traditional notions of explainability and interpretability in machine learning.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This section is structured as follows.
 First,
 Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Local-Expert-Agents-oCELL"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 defines the tripartite structure of local expert agents in oCELL,
 detailing how orthotopic boundaries and local models are mathematically formulated.
 Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Learning-Rules-oCELL"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 then presents the behavioral blueprint of the system through its specific learning rules and the resulting rule table.
 To validate the effectiveness of this approach,
 Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Comparative-Study"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 provides a comparative analysis of oCELL against state-of-the-art ensemble learning algorithms on a two-dimensional regression benchmark.
 Afterwards,
 Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Introspective-Properties-oCELL"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 introduces a set of metrics derived from the spatial distribution of agents,
 demonstrating how the structure of local expert agents can be exploited to monitor the learning and characterize the underlying data distribution.
 Finally,
 Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Limitations"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 presents a critical analysis of oCELL,
 its properties and limitations that subsequently lead to the development of kCELL in Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:kCELL"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

