\begin_layout Subsection
Local Expert Agents
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Local-Expert-Agents-oCELL"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this section,
 the triparttite structure of oCELL's local expert agents is described in detail.
 Each agent 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathcal{A}_{i}=\left\{ \phi_{i},f_{i},\mathcal{M}_{i}\right\} 
\]

\end_inset

 is a specialized local expert that occupies an orthotope-shaped area in the input space,
 maintains a local linear model and manages a private memory of its past experiences.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The activation function 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{i}$
\end_inset

 defines the area of expertise in the input space in which an agent considers itself an expert.
 In oCELL,
 for a 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

-dimensional input space,
 the area of expertise of agent 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{i}$
\end_inset

 is defined by an orthotope 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{i}$
\end_inset

 parameterized by a set of lower bounds 
\begin_inset Formula $l_{i,j}$
\end_inset

 and upper bounds 
\begin_inset Formula $h_{i,j}$
\end_inset

 for each dimension 
\begin_inset Formula $j\in\left\{ 1,\dots,n\right\} $
\end_inset

 such as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathcal{H}_{i}=\left[l_{i,1},h_{i,1}\right]\times\dots\times\left[l_{i,n},h_{i,n}\right]
\]

\end_inset

The hard boundaries defined by the orthotopes allow for the use of geometric operations such as volume measurement or intersection testing.
 The volume 
\begin_inset Formula $V\left(\mathcal{H}_{i}\right)$
\end_inset

 of an orthotope is defined as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
V\left(\mathcal{H}_{i}\right)=\prod_{j=1}^{n}\left(h_{i,j}-l_{i,j}\right)\label{eq:orthotope-volume-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

An observation 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in\mathbb{R}^{n}$
\end_inset

 is considered as intersecting an orthotope 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{i}$
\end_inset

 if,
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\forall j\in\left\{ 1,\dots,n\right\} ,\,\,l_{i,j}\leq x_{i,j}\leq h_{i,j}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Furthermore,
 an orthotope 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{1}$
\end_inset

 intersects another orthotope 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{2}$
\end_inset

 if,
 for all dimension 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\max\left(l_{1,j},l_{2,j}\right)\leq\min\left(h_{1,j},h_{2,j}\right)\label{eq:orthotope-intersection-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $l_{1,j}$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula $l_{2,j}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $h_{1,j}$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula $h_{2,j}$
\end_inset

 denote the lower and upper bounds of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{2}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Agents in oCELL are constructed based on the following assumption of 
\emph on
uniform knowledge
\emph default
:
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
begin{assumption}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

The level of expertise of an agent is assumed to be uniform over its area of expertise for any point intersecting it.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
end{assumption}
\end_layout

\end_inset

Therefore,
 the activation function of an agent 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{i}$
\end_inset

 is a binary function 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{i}:\mathbb{R}^{n}\mapsto\left\{ 0,1\right\} $
\end_inset

 defined as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\phi_{i}\left(x\right)=\begin{cases}
1 & \text{if}\,\,x\,\,\text{intersects}\,\,\mathcal{H}_{i}\\
0 & \text{else}
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

An input 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is said to be In-Distribution (ID) for agent 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{i}$
\end_inset

 if 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{i}\left(x\right)=1$
\end_inset

.
 This hard boundary ensures the agent's area of expertise is well localized and contiguous.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
While the CELL formalism is model-agnostic,
 oCELL is designed to work with low-complexity models to ensure fast updates and convergence of local models in online settings.
 Each agent 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{i}$
\end_inset

 maintains its own local model 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{i}\left(x,\theta_{i}\right)$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{i}$
\end_inset

 denotes the local parameters.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the context of this thesis,
 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{i}$
\end_inset

 is typically a local linear regressor:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{y_{i}}=f_{i}\left(x\right)=\Theta_{i}\left[\begin{array}{c}
x\\
1
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\Theta_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times(n+1)}$
\end_inset

 is the parameter matrix representing the local affine transformation of agent 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{y}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{m}$
\end_inset

 denotes the estimated local prediction.
 With this linear structure,
 each agent provides a first-order approximation of the dynamics within its area of expertise.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The internal state 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{M}_{i}$
\end_inset

 tracks the local history of observations.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{M}_{i}$
\end_inset

 includes a local memory buffer 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{B}_{i}$
\end_inset

 that stores the last observed samples (
\begin_inset Formula $x,y$
\end_inset

).
 This buffer is used to update the internal model 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{i}$
\end_inset

 and has a fixed size to allow forgetting through FIFO (First-In-First-Out) updates.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To transform the population of experts into a global model,
 oCELL uses a spatial gating mechanism to select relevant agents for an input 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
 From a given input 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 the spatial gating mechanism selects two subsets local expert agents:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Activated Agents
\series default
 (
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{\text{activated}}$
\end_inset

) which contains agents for which 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{i}\left(x\right)=1$
\end_inset

 (cf.
 Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:oCELL-Activated"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Neighbor Agents
\series default
 (
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{\text{neighbors}}$
\end_inset

) which contains agents whose orthotope 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{i}$
\end_inset

 intersects a neighborhood orthotope 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{\text{neighborhood}}$
\end_inset

 centered on 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
 The size of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{\text{neighborhood}}$
\end_inset

 is a hyperparameter of the system.
 Tuning this parameter allows the designer to adjust the degree of smoothing in the global prediction.
 (cf.
 Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:oCELL-Neighbors"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The global prediction 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{y}$
\end_inset

 is obtained by an arithmetic mean of the activated agents' proposals
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{y}=\frac{1}{N_{\text{activated}}}\sum_{i\in\mathcal{D}_{\text{activated}}}\hat{y}_{i}
\]

\end_inset

if 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{\text{activated}}$
\end_inset

 is empty,
 the ensemble of neighbor agents 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{\text{neighbors}}$
\end_inset

is selected instead,
 and if 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{\text{neighbors}}$
\end_inset

 is also empty,
 a fallback mechanism selects the 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

-closest agents to ensure continuity in the global approximated function.
 This fallback mechanism prevents prediction failures in regions where the system has limited knowledge (i.e poor paving of the area around 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

).
\end_layout

