\begin_layout Subsection
Learning Rules
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Learning-Rules-oCELL"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since the capabilities and properties of the local expert agents have been described in the previous section,
 this section describes the learning mechanisms and rules that govern the ensemble as a whole to allow the emergence of learning through self-organization.
 In oCELL,
 the evolution of the population is governed by social rules that determine how the ensemble digests the continuous stream of feature-target tuples (
\begin_inset Formula $x_{new},y_{new}$
\end_inset

).
 These rules translate the individual performance of agents and a local social context into discrete actions:
 updating an agent's model,
 adapting its shape or managing the population through creation and destruction of agents.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To determine the appropriate action to apply,
 each agent 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{i}$
\end_inset

 selected by the spatial gating mechanism (where 
\begin_inset Formula $i\in\mathcal{D}_{\text{selected}}$
\end_inset

) receives a feedback signal based on the quality 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{\mathcal{A}_{i}}$
\end_inset

 of its proposition of prediction.
 This is typically calculated as the squared error (any other error function could be used) between the local proposal 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{y}_{i}$
\end_inset

 and the true observation 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
L_{\mathcal{A}_{i}}=\left\Vert \hat{y}_{i}-y\right\Vert ^{2}
\]

\end_inset

These errors are categorized into three regimes using two sensitivity thresholds 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau_{\text{good}}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau_{\text{bad}}$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Good Prediction
\series default
:
 The agent is a reliable expert for this input (
\begin_inset Formula $L_{\mathcal{A}_{i}}\in\left[0,\tau_{\text{good}}\right]$
\end_inset

)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Inaccurate Prediction
\series default
:
 The agent is relevant but requires model refinement (
\begin_inset Formula $L_{\mathcal{A}_{i}}\in\left[\tau_{\text{good}},\tau_{\text{bad}}\right]$
\end_inset

)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Bad Prediction
\series default
:
 The agent's is not relevant for this region (
\begin_inset Formula $L_{\mathcal{A}_{i}}\in\left[\tau_{\text{bad}},+\infty\right[$
\end_inset

)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The learning rules are triggered based on the social context defined by the number of activated agents (
\begin_inset Formula $N_{\text{activated}}=\left|\mathcal{D}_{\text{activated}}\right|$
\end_inset

) and neighbors (
\begin_inset Formula $N_{\text{neighbors}}=\left|\mathcal{D}_{\text{neighbors}}\right|$
\end_inset

).
 The following paragraphs present the various social contexts that are considered.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Inaccuracy
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{\text{activated}}\geq1$
\end_inset

,
 that 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{new}$
\end_inset

 is already covered by local expert agents.
 Activated agents that provide 
\emph on
good
\emph default
 prediction will peform a 
\series bold
Shape Update
\series default
 action to expand their area of expertise in the direction of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{new}$
\end_inset

 with the aim of generalizing their expertise.
 Activated agent that provide 
\emph on
inaccurate
\emph default
 prediction perform a 
\series bold
Model Update
\series default
 to refine their local expertise.
 Activated agents that provide 
\emph on
bad
\emph default
 prediction perform a 
\series bold
Shape Update 
\series default
to retract their area of expertise away from 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{new}$
\end_inset

 with the aim of excluding that point,
 specializing the boundaries to maintain local accuracy.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Incompetence
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{\text{activated}}=0$
\end_inset

 their is a lack of relevant expert to predict for 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{new}$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{\text{neighbors}}\geq1$
\end_inset

,
 the neighbor agents are candidate to cover the void,
 i.e to become activated for 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{new}$
\end_inset

.
 Neighbor agents that provide 
\emph on
good
\emph default
 or 
\emph on
inaccurate
\emph default
 prediction expand their area of expertise toward 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{new}$
\end_inset

 (
\series bold
Shape Update
\series default
) and update their internal model (
\series bold
Model Update
\series default
).
 However,
 if all neighbors provide 
\emph on
bad
\emph default
 prediction (no satisfactory prediction) or if 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{\text{neighbors}}=0$
\end_inset

,
 an 
\series bold
Agent Creation
\series default
 meta-action is triggered.
 A new local expert agent centered on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{new}$
\end_inset

 is spawned into the ensemble with initial dimensions defined by an hyperparameter 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Uselessness
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When applying too many 
\series bold
Shape Update
\series default
 actions involving retraction,
 the area of expertise can evolve into a degenerate shape,
 becoming far too small to be informative.
 To prevent uncontrolled population growth and eliminate degenerate agents a destruction rule is applied.
 Any local expert agent whose volume 
\begin_inset Formula $V\left(\mathcal{H}_{i}\right)$
\end_inset

 falls below a threshold 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau_{\text{vol}}$
\end_inset

is removed from the ensemble (
\series bold
Agent Destruction
\series default
).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:Learning-rules-of-oCELL"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 provide the behavioral blueprint of oCELL mapping different social contexts and conditions to actions and meta-actions allowing self-organization.
 The behavior of oCELL is parameterized by a set of hyperparameters that influence local convergence and stability of the emergent model:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\tau_{\text{good}},\tau_{\text{bad}}$
\end_inset

:
 define the error tolerance of local experts.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

:
 the volume variation coefficient controlling the plasticity of the area of expertise of agents.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

:
 the initial dimensions for new created.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\tau_{\text{vol}}$
\end_inset

:
 the parsimony threshold on volume for agent destruction.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
memory length:
 the maximum number of data points that can be stored into 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{B}_{i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

