\begin_layout Subsection
Discussion and Limitations of oCELL
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Limitations"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The previous sections established the oCELL instantiation of the CELL formalism,
 demonstrating that a population of self-organizing local expert agents can achieve predictive performance comparable to state-of-the-art ensemble algorithms on low dimensional regression tasks while offering a rich set of introspection metrics.
 In this section,
 these results are discussed in light of the global objectives of this thesis and critically examine the structural limitations of oCELL that motivate the transition toward smoother representations.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the comparative study presented in Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Comparative-Study"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 oCELL achieves competitive accuracy levels using only local linear models,
 demonstrating that global modeling complexity effectively arise from social interactions within the ensemble of local expert agents instead of solely relying on high-capacity internal models.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The introspective metrics that were derived from oCELL in the previous section offer valuable tools for analysis of the internal structures of the ensemble of agents.
 We argue that they are linked to the traditional notions of explainability and interpretability in machine learning.
 To situate oCELL within the field of eXplainable Artificial Intelligence (XAI),
 the explainability and interpretability denominations should be defined beforehand.
 Following 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "burkart2021survey"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 we define 
\emph on
interpretability
\emph default
 as the inherent ability to understand how the model works as a whole,
 focusing on the model's structure and mechanisms,
 i.e transparency.
 In contrast,
 
\emph on
explainability
\emph default
 is associated with the methodologies used to communicate the reasoning for a specific decision taken by a model.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As oCELL is designed,
 it acts as a white-box model.
 Unlike black-box models (e.g.
 Neural Networks in Deep Learning) with opaque inner workings,
 oCELL uses linear regression models for prediction and orthotopes for spatialization through activation functions,
 which have simpler less opaque structure.
 The orthotopes provide a clear geometric representation of the areas of expertise.
 They act as symbolic 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

if-then
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 human-interpretable rules where each feature dimension can be manipulated independently.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The introspection metrics introduced in previous section reveal capabilities that can be leveraged for fault detection,
 model debugging or smart resampling strategies.
 In a robotic controller,
 these metrics act as operational safeguards.
 Indeed,
 being able to quantify epistemic uncertainty through the Coverage Index (
\begin_inset Formula $p_{\text{coverage}}$
\end_inset

) for example,
 allows to evaluate the reliability of the model for a given input.
 When the model is not reliable,
 the controller should switch to a simpler more robust control strategy to ensure safety.
 In addition to that,
 a high degree of discrepancy 
\begin_inset Formula $\kappa$
\end_inset

 value acts as a warning sign that the current local modelisation of the ensemble of agents may be inaccurate due to hard-to-learn dynamics.
 Finally,
 Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:Summary-Metrics-Explainability-oCELL"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 summarizes the scopes and links between the defined metrics and the notions of explainability and interpretability.
\end_layout

