\begin_layout Standard
Alongside transparency,
 the multi-agent nature of oCELL provides functional advanatges for regression.
 Unlike previous works on mutli-agent systems based on an ensemble of local expert agents 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "fourez2024analyzing"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 oCELL embraces agent overlaps instead of avoiding them.
 We argue that for continuous regression tasks,
 these overlaps are highly meaningful as the consensus derived from multiple local experts helps to smooth the approximated function.
 This smoothness is an interesting property for using oCELL in Model Predictive Control (MPC),
 especially when using gradient-based optimizers that require smooth transitions between local models.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Despite those encouraging results,
 several structural bottlenecks were identified during the experimental phase.
 These limitations mainly highlight a tension between the simplicity of the orthotope representation of areas of expertise and the complexity of real-world data manifolds.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A primary limitation lies in the 
\emph on
uniform knowledge 
\emph default
hypothesis on which oCELL is based.
 oCELL assumes an agent is equally competent across its entire area of expertise delimited by an orthotope (
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{i}$
\end_inset

).
 In practice,
 the reliability of an agent typically decays toward its boundaries.
 Currently,
 agents in oCELL have binary activation functions,
 meaning that any activated agent is weighted equally in their contribution to the final prediction.
 In this work,
 weighting approachs based on Euclidean distance to the agent's centroid were explored.
 However,
 it did not yield significant improvements.
 This suggests that a more nuanced weighting should account for both the agent's centroid and the specific geometric support of its area of expertise.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Using orthotopes is computationally efficient for shape update operations like expansion or retraction but are limited to represent faithfully complex data manifolds.
 As illustrated in Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Limitation orthotope agents"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 (side by side comparison of an overgeneralizing agent and a representative agent by visualizing their respective orthotopes (represented by a blue rectangle) and training data (represented by red dots)),
 the area of expertise of an agent may occupy a large orthotope while its training data populate only a small manifold (i.e a subregion),
 causing abusive expertise claims as the model can report high coverage index (
\begin_inset Formula $p_{\text{coverage}}$
\end_inset

) in regions where it has never actually seen data.
 Accurate spatialization and potentially richer base shapes is therefore needed to capture local data structures and prevent overgeneralization.
\end_layout

