\begin_layout Standard
A local expert agent is spatialized in the input space by the mean 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}$
\end_inset

 and covariance matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}$
\end_inset

 of the distribution of its training points.
 The activation function 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{i}:\mathbb{R}^{n}\mapsto\left]0,1\right]$
\end_inset

 for a given input 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in\mathbb{R}^{n}$
\end_inset

 is given by the RBF kernel value
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\phi_{i}\left(x\right)=\exp\left(-\frac{d\left(\mathcal{A}_{i},x\right)^{2}}{2l^{2}}\right)
\]

\end_inset

with 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 the lengthscale of the kernel and 
\begin_inset Formula $d\left(\mathcal{A}_{i},x\right)$
\end_inset

 a measure of similarity between an agent 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{i}$
\end_inset

 and an input 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
 In kCELL this similarity metric is defined as the Mahalanobis distance such as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
d\left(\mathcal{A}_{i},x\right)=\sqrt{\left(x-\mu_{i}\right)^{\top}\Sigma_{i}^{-1}\left(x-\mu_{i}\right)}
\]

\end_inset

In this context,
 the activation score can be interpreted as the likelihood that the agent has previously been trained on points similar to 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

,
 reflecting its degree of knowledge.
 Contrary to oCELL,
 the knowledge uniformity hypothesis is discarded.
 Expertise is assumed to be maximized on the center of the agent's area of expertise.
 Even with this continuous representation,
 some measurable mathematical quantities of oCELL can be translated to kCELL such as the notion of volume which remains relevant.
 As the area of expertise of an agent is not clearly bounded,
 its volume is tied to the volume of a confidence ellipsoid.
 The volume 
\begin_inset Formula $V\left(\mathcal{A}_{i}\right)$
\end_inset

 is proportional to the square root of the determinant of the covariance matrix 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "williams2006gaussian"
literal "false"

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
V\left(\mathcal{A}_{i}\right)\propto\sqrt{\det\left(\Sigma_{i}\right)}
\]

\end_inset

As an instantiation of the CELL formalism,
 kCELL is model-agnostic but is designed to work with low-complexity models to ensure fast updates and local convergence.
 Each agent 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{i}$
\end_inset

 maintains a local linear regressor 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{i}$
\end_inset

 to predict
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{y}_{i}=f_{i}\left(x\right)=\Theta_{i}\left[\begin{array}{c}
x\\
1
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\Theta_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times\left(n+1\right)}$
\end_inset

 is the parameter matrix representing the local affine transformation.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The internal state 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{M}_{i}$
\end_inset

 tracks the number of point 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{\text{seen}}$
\end_inset

 that have been used to update the shape of each local expert agent.
 Unlike oCELL's buffer memory,
 kCELL is designed to be lightweight by retaining as few points as possible.
 Additionally,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{M}_{i}$
\end_inset

 stores a running confidence value 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{C}_{\mathcal{A}_{i}}\in\left[-1,1\right]$
\end_inset

 which corresponds to an exponential moving average of the normalized social feedbacks received by the agent.
 This value tracks the agent's predictive performance relative to its peers and is used for the destruction mechanism.
 The precise feedback mechanism is detailed in Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:kCELL-Learning-Rules"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To transform the population of local expert agents into a global model,
 kCELL uses a spatial gating mechanism to select relevant agents for a given input 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
 Unlike oCELL,
 the continuous nature of the activation makes the notion of activated agents blurry.
 For this reason,
 the spatial gating mechanism focuses on selecting one subset of local expert agents:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Neighbor Agents
\series default
 (
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{\text{neighbors}}$
\end_inset

):
 which contains agents for which 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is likely to belong to the training dataset,
 such as 
\begin_inset Formula $d\left(\mathcal{A}_{i},x\right)^{2}\leq\chi_{n,\frac{\xi}{100}}^{2}$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\chi_{n,\frac{\xi}{100}}^{2}$
\end_inset

 denotes the 
\begin_inset Formula $\xi$
\end_inset

th percentile of the chi-squared distribution with 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 degrees of freedom.
 The value of 
\begin_inset Formula $\xi$
\end_inset

 belongs to the hyperparameters of the system that needs to be set beforehand.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As stated earlier,
 kCELL discards the knowledge uniformity hypothesis to better represent the knowledge encapsulated in the ensemble of local expert agents.
 Through the defined activation function,
 the further a point is from the agent's center,
 the lower its activation value.
 In this continuous context,
 local expert agents should have an impact on the global prediction that is proportional to their degree of expertise about the input.
 The global prediction 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{y}$
\end_inset

 is obtained through a soft-weighted prediction mechanism.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{k}$
\end_inset

 denote the index set of the 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

-closest agents in feature space.
 The final prediction is given by 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{y}=\sum_{i\in\mathcal{D}_{k}}w_{i}\left(x\right)f_{i}\left(x\right)
\]

\end_inset

where the normalized contribution weights 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

 are defined as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
w_{i}\left(x\right)=\frac{\phi_{i}\left(x\right)}{\sum_{j\in\mathcal{D}_{k}}\phi_{j}\left(x\right)}
\]

\end_inset

This formulation ensures that each contributing agent's influence on the final prediction is proportional to its estimated competence.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Shape Update
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In kCELL,
 the shape update operation is expressed purely as a mean and covariance estimation problem.
 Each time a point is ingested by agent 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{i}$
\end_inset

,
 its mean 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{i}$
\end_inset

 and covariance matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{i}$
\end_inset

 are updated incrementally using Welford's algorithm 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "welford1962note"
literal "false"

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\mu_{i\,|\,t+1} & = & \mu_{i\,|\,t}+\frac{x-\mu_{i\,|\,t}}{n_{\text{seen}}+1}\\
\Sigma_{i\,|\,t+1} & = & \frac{1}{n_{\text{seen}}}\left(\Sigma_{i\,|\,t}+\left(x-\mu_{i\,|\,t}\right)\left(x-\mu_{i\,|\,t+1}\right)^{\top}\right)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{\text{seen}}$
\end_inset

 is the number of points used by the agent to update its shape.
 This allows the agents to adapt their area of expertise incrementally without maintaining a buffer of past observations.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Note Note
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The spatial parameters of the activation function of agents,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{i}$
\end_inset

 are updated incrementally using Welford's algorithm 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "welford1962note"
literal "false"

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\mu_{i\,|\,t+1} & = & \mu_{i\,|\,t}+\frac{x-\mu_{i\,|\,t}}{n_{\text{seen}}+1}\\
\Sigma_{i\,|\,t+1} & = & \frac{1}{n_{\text{seen}}}\left(\Sigma_{i\,|\,t+1}+\left(x-\mu_{i\,|\,t}\right)\left(x-\mu_{i\,|\,t+1}\right)^{\top}\right)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{\text{seen}}$
\end_inset

 is the number of points used by the agent to update its shape.
 With this approach,
 the Shape Update operation is expressed purely as a mean and covariance estimation problem,
 grounded in a clear statistical meaning.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Model Update
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
An agent 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{i}$
\end_inset

 can update its internal model.
 To ensure efficiency in online settings and avoid the need for a memory buffer,
 the parameter matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\Theta_{i}$
\end_inset

 is updated using Recursive Least Squares (RLS) 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "haykin2002adaptive"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 RLS naturally handles the notion of forgetting and for a forgetting value of 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

 (i.e no forgetting),
 it converges rapidly to producing the same results as Ordinary Least Squares (OLS).
 This way,
 the agents provide a first-order approximation of the dynamics that converges as new data is ingested sequentially from the data stream.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Note Note
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
To ensure fast and accurate updates while avoiding to maintain a memory buffer 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{B}_{i}$
\end_inset

,
 the parameter matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\Theta_{i}$
\end_inset

 is updated using Recursive Least Squares (RLS) 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "haykin2002adaptive"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 RLS naturally handles the notion of forgetting and for a forgetting value of 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

 (i.e no forgetting),
 it converges rapidly to producing the same results as Ordinary Least Squares (OLS).
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Note Note
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
===================================================================
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Note Note
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
As for oCELL 
\begin_inset Note Comment
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Ref to definition of context agents in oCELL
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 the main entities of kCELL are the Context agents.
 A Context agent 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{i}$
\end_inset

 is characterized by an activation function 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{i}\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

 and a prediction function 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{i}\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

 such as 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathcal{A}_{i}=\left\{ \phi_{i},f_{i}\right\} 
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
However,
 unlike in oCELL,
 the Context agents of kCELL are spatialized differently.
 In oCELL,
 a binary activation (the point is inside or outside the associated orthotope) is used while in kCELL,
 a RBF kernel is used as a continuous and smooth activation function to represent the area of expertise in feature space.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
First,
 the spatialization mechanism of context agents based on RBF kernels is described.
 Then the soft weighted prediction mechanism allowing for weighting based on estimated level of expertise is presented.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Kernel Spatialization
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Kernel-Spatialization"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
In kCELL,
 contrary to using orthotopes a RBF kernel is used as the activation function to unlock more degrees of freedom to represent the areas of expertise 
\begin_inset Note Comment
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Add ref to illustration Figure
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 This activation function is smooth and differentiable,
 making the system more optimization-friendly to be used for control tasks as a dynamics model.
 Therefore,
 an agent is spatialized by the mean 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}$
\end_inset

 and covariance matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}$
\end_inset

 of the distribution of its training points.
 Since this activation function has statistical significance,
 it is easier to define the neighborhood as a confidence interval whose confidence value can be adjusted.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Float figure
placement document
alignment document
wide false
sideways false
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Caption Standard

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Comparison between area of expertise oCELL vs kCELL
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
The distance between a point 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in\mathbb{R}^{n}$
\end_inset

 and a Context agent 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{i}$
\end_inset

 is defined as the mahalanobis distance:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
d\left(\mathcal{A}_{i},x\right)=D_{M}\left(\mu_{i},\Sigma_{i},x\right)=\sqrt{\left(x-\mu_{i}\right)^{\top}\Sigma_{i}^{-1}\left(x-\mu_{i}\right)}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Separator plain
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
A Context agent 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{i}$
\end_inset

 is considered as a neighbor of 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in\mathbb{R}^{n}$
\end_inset

 if 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is likely to belong to the training dataset of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{i}$
\end_inset

 such as 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
D_{M}\left(\mu_{i},\Sigma_{i},x\right)^{2}\leq\chi_{n,0.95}^{2}
\]

\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\chi_{n,0.95}^{2}$
\end_inset

 denotes the 
\begin_inset Formula $95$
\end_inset

th percentile of the chi-squared distribution with 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 degrees of freedom.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Separator plain
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
The activation function 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{i}:\mathbb{R}^{n}\mapsto\left]0,1\right]$
\end_inset

 of a Context agent 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{i}$
\end_inset

 for a point 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in\mathbb{R}^{n}$
\end_inset

 is given by the RBF kernel value
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\phi_{i}\left(\mathcal{A}_{i},x\right)=\exp\left(-\frac{d\left(\mathcal{A}_{i},x\right)}{2l^{2}}\right)
\]

\end_inset

with 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 the lengthscale of the kernel.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
The activation score of a point 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 for a given agent 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{i}$
\end_inset

 can be interpreted as the likelihood that the agent has previously been trained on points similar to 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 and thus reflects the agent's degree of knowledge over the corresponding region of feature space.
 We can draw a parallel between this activation function based on RBF kernel and the coverage index 
\begin_inset Note Comment
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Insert ref to coverage index in previous section
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 an explainability metric derived from oCELL which is also linked to the degree of knowledge of a point.
 In kCELL,
 this explainability property related to the knowledge of a point emerges naturally from the definition of the system.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
One of the requirements of kCELL is to be lightweight,
 meaning that as few points as possible should be retained in memory to truly represent information through local models.
 As previously stated,
 the spatialization of each agent can be fully described by a mean 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{i}$
\end_inset

 and a covariance matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{i}$
\end_inset

 which are the parameters of the activation function 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Each time a point is ingested by agent 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{i}$
\end_inset

,
 its mean and covariance matrix are updated using the Welford's algorithm 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "welford1962note"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 such as 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\mu_{i\,|\,t+1} & = & \mu_{i\,|\,t}+\frac{x-\mu_{i\,|\,t}}{n+1}\\
\Sigma_{i\,|\,t+1} & = & \frac{1}{n}\left(\Sigma_{i\,|\,t+1}+\left(x-\mu_{i\,|\,t}\right)\left(x-\mu_{i\,|\,t+1}\right)^{\top}\right)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 is the number of points ingested by the agent to update its shape.
 With this approach the shape change is expressed purely as a mean and covariance estimation problem.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
The notion of volume expressed in Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Context-Agents"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 (cf.
 Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:orthotope-volume"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) still carries on.
 The volume of an agent is tied to the volume of a confidence ellipsoid defined by a theshold on squared mahalanobis distance.
 Thus the volume of an agent is proportional to the square root of the determinant of the associated covariance matrix
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
v\left(\mathcal{A}_{i}\right)\propto\sqrt{\det\left(\Sigma_{i}\right)}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Soft-Weighted Prediction
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsubsec:Soft-Weighted-Prediction"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
In kCELL,
 we get rid of the knowledge uniformity hypothesis of agents to better represent the knowledge of the system.
 Therefore the activation function of a context agent materializes its area of expertise through a smooth RBF kernel.
 The further a point is from the agent's centroid,
 the lower its activation value.
 This is because we assume that expertise is maximized at the agent's center.
 Agents with higher activation values contribute more heavily to the final output because they are more expert than others at predicting.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{k}=\left\{ i|\mathcal{A}_{i}\,\text{is in the \ensuremath{k}-closest to}\,x\right\} $
\end_inset

 denote the index set of the 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 nearest agents in feature space.
 The final prediction 
\begin_inset Formula $f\left(x\right)\in\mathbb{R}^{m}$
\end_inset

 of the system is then given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f\left(x\right)=\sum_{i\in S_{k}}w_{i}\left(x\right)f_{i}\left(x\right)
\]

\end_inset

where the normalized contribution weights 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

 are defined as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
w_{i}\left(x\right)=\frac{\phi_{i}\left(x\right)}{\sum_{j\in S_{k}}\phi_{j}\left(x\right)}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
This formulation ensures that each contributing agent's influence on the final prediction is proportional to its estimated competence.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Learning Rules
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:kCELL-Learning-Rules"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The previous section detailed the tripartite structure of local expert agents in kCELL that are spatialized using continuous smooth RBF kernels.
 This section follows this line of thought,
 describing the social learning rules that govern the collextive behavior of the ensemble.
 While oCELL relies on binary coverage and fixed error thresholds,
 kCELL introduces a feedback mechanism based on social relative performance and baseline benchmarking allowing the agents to 
\emph on
cooperate
\emph default
 to refine the global prediction while 
\emph on
competing
\emph default
 for social survival.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As described in the CELL formalism,
 the self-organization of the ensemble of local expert agents in kCELL is governed by rules that determine how the population digests the sequential stream of feature-target tuples (
\begin_inset Formula $x_{new},y_{new}$
\end_inset

).
 These rules translate the individual performance of agents within a specific social context into discrete actions:
 updating an agent's model,
 adapting its shape or managing the population through creation and destruction.
 In order to track an agent's reliability over time,
 kCELL leverages two types of feedback signals that assess the quality of an agent's contribution.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Ensemble Feedback (
\begin_inset Formula $N_{\text{neighbors}}>1$
\end_inset

)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When multiple agents are neighbors of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{new}$
\end_inset

,
 they must cooperate locally to refine the approximate of the group.
 The ensemble feedback is defined as the fractional change in error 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta_{\mathcal{A}_{i}}$
\end_inset

 when leaving agent 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{i}$
\end_inset

 out of the global prediction process
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Delta_{\mathcal{A}_{i}}=\frac{E_{-i}-E}{E}=\frac{\left|\hat{y}_{-i}-y_{new}\right|-\left|\hat{y}-y_{new}\right|}{\left|\hat{y}-y_{new}\right|}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $E$
\end_inset

 is the prediction error and 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{-i}=\left|\hat{y}_{-i}-y_{new}\right|$
\end_inset

 is error calculated without 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{i}$
\end_inset

's proposal.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta_{\mathcal{A}_{i}}$
\end_inset

 indicates that the agent is a 
\emph on
strong
\emph default
 expert contributing to error reduction while 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta_{\mathcal{A}_{i}}<0$
\end_inset

 identifies 
\emph on
weak
\emph default
 experts.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Baseline Feedback (
\begin_inset Formula $N_{\text{neighbors}}\leq1$
\end_inset

)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When an agent is alone or no neighbors exist,
 social comparison is impossible.
 Instead,
 the feedback 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta_{\mathcal{A}_{i}}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 is defined in such a way as to compare to a baseline prediction 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{base}$
\end_inset

 provided by a running short-term linear predictor that is regularly fitted with Ordinary Least Squares (OLS) on a sliding window buffer 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{B}_{\text{short}}$
\end_inset

 of the most recent observation of the system,
 such as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Delta_{\mathcal{A}_{i}}^{\prime}=\frac{E_{base}-E_{i}}{E_{base}}=\frac{\left|y_{base}-y_{new}\right|-\left|\hat{y}_{i}-y_{new}\right|}{\left|y_{base}-y_{new}\right|}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{base}$
\end_inset

 is the prediction error of the short term linear predictor,
 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{i}=\left|\hat{y}_{i}-y_{new}\right|$
\end_inset

 is the prediction error of agent 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 This value assesses whether the agent is more relevant than a simple local trend.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
These feedback signals are used to update the running confidence 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{C}_{\mathcal{A}_{i}}$
\end_inset

 stored in the agent's state memory 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{M}_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 First,
 an instantaneous confidence 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}\in\left[-1,1\right]$
\end_inset

 is computed
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
c_{i}=\tanh\left(k\times\begin{cases}
\Delta_{\mathcal{A}_{i}} & \text{if}\,\,N>1\\
\Delta_{\mathcal{A}_{i}}^{\prime} & \text{else}
\end{cases}\right)
\]

\end_inset

The running confidence is then updated from the value of 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 via an exponential moving average
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\bar{C}_{\mathcal{A}_{i}\,|\,t+1}=\left(1-\lambda\right)\bar{C}_{\mathcal{A}_{i}\,|\,t}+\lambda c_{\mathcal{A}_{i}}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

 is the smoothing factor,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{C}_{\mathcal{A}_{i}\,|\,t}$
\end_inset

 is the current value of the running confidence and 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{C}_{\mathcal{A}_{i}\,|\,t+1}$
\end_inset

 is the next one.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The learning rules are triggered by conditions on the social context and the feedback signals.
 These rules allow local groups of agents to continuously improve by refining weak agents while keeping them mobile and locally adaptable,
 and specializing strong agents within their current area of expertise.
 The following paragraphs detail the learning rules.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Inaccuracy
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{\text{neighbors}}>1$
\end_inset

,
 several agents are theoretically considered as experts to predict for 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{new}$
\end_inset

.
 However,
 each one of them possesses a different level of expertise for 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{new}$
\end_inset

 depending on the value of their activation function 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{i}\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

.
 The social feedback 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta_{\mathcal{A}_{i}}$
\end_inset

 distinguishes those who are currently stronger from those who are weaker.
 Weaker agents (
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta_{\mathcal{A}_{i}}<0$
\end_inset

) perform a 
\series bold
Model Update
\series default
 action to refine their local expertise.
 Conversely,
 strong agents (
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta_{\mathcal{A}_{i}}>0$
\end_inset

) are already performing well and do a 
\series bold
Shape Update
\series default
 action to strengthen their local anchoring and improve the collective knowledge.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Specialization
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{\text{neighbors}}=1$
\end_inset

,
 the only neighbor agent cannot be compared to other experts.
 In this case,
 it is compared against a short-term linear baseline predictor.
 If the agent outperforms the baseline (
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta_{\mathcal{A}_{i}}^{\prime}>0$
\end_inset

),
 it is considered a relevant local expert and performs both a 
\series bold
Model Update
\series default
 and a 
\series bold
Shape Update
\series default
 action.
 This dual update allows the agent to specialize in its current region by refining its parameters while moving its area of expertise closer to 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{new}$
\end_inset

.
 Converselt,
 it the agent is beaten by the baseline (
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta_{\mathcal{A}_{i}}^{\prime}<0$
\end_inset

),
 it remains idle,
 waiting to reposition itself through future points or for another agent to enter the area to initiate social cooperation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Incompetence
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{\text{neighbors}}=0$
\end_inset

,
 no agent can be considered as a neighbor of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{new}$
\end_inset

.
 The learning system identifies a lack of local knowledge around 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{new}$
\end_inset

.
 In this context,
 the feedback 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta_{\mathcal{A}_{i}}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 is calculated for the closest agent within the ensemble.
 If this agent outperforms the short-term linear baseline (
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta_{\mathcal{A}_{i}}^{\prime}>0$
\end_inset

),
 it performs a 
\series bold
Model Update
\series default
 and a 
\series bold
Shape Update
\series default
 to extend its area of expertise toward 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{new}$
\end_inset

 and include the point.
 However,
 if the closest agent is outperformed by the baseline (
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta_{\mathcal{A}_{i}}^{\prime}<0$
\end_inset

),
 an 
\series bold
Agent Creation
\series default
 meta-action is triggered.
 A new local expert agent is spawned,
 centered on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{new}$
\end_inset

.
 To accelerate local convergence and avoid initializing with a single point,
 the new agent is initialized from a short-term sliding window buffer 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{B}_{\text{ini}}$
\end_inset

containing the 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{\text{ini}}$
\end_inset

 most recent observations seen by the system.
 The buffer 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{B}_{\text{ini}}$
\end_inset

 is cleared at each 
\series bold
Agent Creation
\series default
 action to avoid redundant agent creation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Uselessness 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To prevent uncontrolled population growth and mitigate the proliferation of agents that do not contribute to predictive accuracy,
 a destruction mechanism based on social survival is used.
 Unliked oCELL which uses a volume-based threshold,
 kCELL relies on the running confidence 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{C}_{\mathcal{A}_{i}}$
\end_inset

 stored in 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{M}_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 If an agent consistently receives negative feedbacks by either being the weakest of a local group or by failing to beat the short-term baseline,
 its confidence will gradually decrease.
 When 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{C}_{\mathcal{A}_{i}}$
\end_inset

 falls below a predefined threshold 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau_{\text{confidence}}$
\end_inset

,
 the agent is considered useless and an 
\series bold
Agent Destruction
\series default
 action is triggered.
 This ensures that the ensemble of local expert agents remains efficient and that only the most reliable experts survive.
\end_layout

