\begin_layout Plain Layout
Learning rules of kCELL
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "tab:Learning-rules-of-kCELL"

\end_inset

.
 The far left column corresponds to the title of the paragraph in which the rules are presented.
 Each line corresponds to a given learning rule associated with a social context representing a condition on the number of local experts 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{\text{neighbors}}$
\end_inset

 around a new input 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{new}$
\end_inset

;
 with a condition on one of the two types of feedback 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta_{\mathcal{A}_{i}}$
\end_inset

and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta_{\mathcal{A}_{i}}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 or on the running confidence of agents 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{C}_{\mathcal{A}_{i}}$
\end_inset

;
 with a target subset of agents that will be affected by the rule (e.g Closest the subset of agents containing only the agent which is closest to 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{new}$
\end_inset

);
 with actions or meta-actions to perform on the agents of the targeted subset.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset

To visualize the result of a learning and local cooperation of agents,
 a 1-dimensional example can be used.
 A small synthetic dataset is generated from the function 
\begin_inset Formula $f\left(x\right)=\sin\left(x\right)+\epsilon\,\mathcal{N}\left(0,1\right)$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon\in\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 the noise scaling factor.
 Then,
 online learning is simulated by sequentially feeding the feature-target tuples (
\begin_inset Formula $x_{new},y_{new}$
\end_inset

) one at a time to kCELL.
 Each point is only seen once during learning.
 Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:1D-Visualization-of-local-coop"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 presents a side-by-side visualization of the spatial organization of agents 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "fig:1D-Spatial-organization"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 against the result of aggregating their predictions 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "fig:1D-Resulting-prediction"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

.
 We can observe that the agents overlap and do not fit perfectly across their entire area of expertise;
 at the edges of agents the fit seems to have more errors and conversely on the center.
 This is the expected behavior resulting from the assumption of non-uniformity in the knowledge within areas of expertise.
 We observe that the overall predictions approximate closely the function considering the noise added to the data.
 It demonstrates kCELL's ability to generalize effectively by interpolation.
 This highlights the usefulness of a soft-weighted aggregation function.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Finally,
 implementation-wise,
 the behavior of learning in kCELL is parameterized by the following hyperparameters:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\xi$
\end_inset

:
 the chi-squared percentile defining a threshold for the gating mechanism to extract the subset of neighbor agents from the global population.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

:
 the steepness of the tanh function for social feedback normalization.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

:
 the smoothing factor for the running exponential moving average of confidence value of agents.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\tau_{\text{confidence}}$
\end_inset

:
 the survival threshold for agent destructuction.
 If an agent's confidence goes below this value it is destroyed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $N_{\text{short}}$
\end_inset

:
 the size of the 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{B}_{\text{short}}$
\end_inset

 used to fit the linear baseline predictor.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $N_{\text{ini}}$
\end_inset

:
 the minimal number of points inside 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{B}_{\text{ini}}$
\end_inset

 needed to be able to create a new agent.
\end_layout

