\begin_layout Standard
kCELL is compared against several standard online learning algorithms:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Adaptive Hoeffding Trees
\series default
:
 An online decision tree variant.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Mondrian Forest
\series default
:
 A highly efficient adaptation of forest tree ensembles for online learning.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Linear Regression
\series default
:
 Optimized with Adam optimizer 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "kingma2017adammethodstochasticoptimization"
literal "false"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
k-Nearest Neighbors (kNN)
\series default
:
 a lazy local learning algorithm.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Locally Weighted Projection Regression (LWPR)
\series default
:
 The most direct competitor of kCELL as it uses a similar local linear model spatialization but employs different learning logic.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For implementation of Adpative Hoeffding Trees,
 Mondrian Forest,
 Linear Regression with Adam optimizer and kNN th python library RiverML is used 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "montiel2021river"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 For LWPR,
 the original python C-bindings are used.
 Performing an exhaustive hyperparameter optimization on the whole dataset is impracticable because it is too computationally heavy.
 For this reason,
 hyperparameter optimization was conducted via grid search on the first 5000 steps of the stream to minimize the Area Under the Curve (AUC) of the prequential error.
 The prequential error corresponds to the error measured for a new point of the data stream before learning on it.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The performance of the models is evaluated through adaptation speed,
 measured by prequential error,
 and asynptotic convergence measured by the Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE) and Mean Absolute Error (MAE) on the fixed test set.
\end_layout

