\begin_layout Paragraph
Structural Evolution of Agent Population
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To better understand these adpatation patterns,
 the evolution of the local expert agent population displayed in Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Evolution-of-nb-agents"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 is analyzed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Inverted Pendulum
\series default
:
 Each dynamic change induces a short-term surge in the number of agents following by a reduction of this number and a stabilization.
 This indicates a restructuration of the population.
 New dynamics trigger agent creation while the confidence-based mechanism prunes older agents that are no longer relevant to the current dynamics.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Hopper
\series default
:
 The number of agents grows monotonically.
 Distinct growth regimes are observed for each gravity value.
 It starts with a logarithmic growth for 
\begin_inset Formula $g=9.81$
\end_inset

,
 followed by a linear growth for 
\begin_inset Formula $g=4$
\end_inset

,
 and finally it starts to stabilize around 20000 steps for 
\begin_inset Formula $g=20$
\end_inset

.
 This suggests an accumulation of local expert agents rather than a replacement of the existing population.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Note Note
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
