\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Certifiability goes beyond basic testing by requiring comprehensive evidence of functional safety,
 cybersecurity,
 and intended functionality across diverse scenarios.
 It addresses the complexity of ADAS,
 where sensors,
 AI algorithms,
 and actuators interact in unpredictable real-world conditions,
 demanding standards like ISO 26262 (functional safety) and ISO/PAS 21448 (SOTIF for safety of intended functionality).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Functional Safety
\series default
 (ISO 26262):
 Systems must achieve Automotive Safety Integrity Levels (ASIL) through hazard analysis,
 risk assessment (HARA),
 and traceability from requirements to tests,
 including hardware/software audits and fault-tolerant design.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Scenario Validation
\series default
:
 Extensive testing in simulations,
 hardware-in-the-loop (HIL),
 closed tracks,
 and real-world conditions covers operational design domains (ODDs),
 edge cases,
 corner cases,
 and environmental stressors like fog or high traffic.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Performance and Compliance
\series default
:
 Features must meet UN regulations (e.g.,
 UN R171 for driver control assistance systems) with KPIs for metrics like time-to-collision,
 braking profiles,
 driver monitoring,
 and human-machine interfaces.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Process and Lifecycle Audits
\series default
:
 Parallel certification of development processes,
 production conformity,
 software updates (including OTA),
 and end-of-life procedures,
 with documentation for type approval authorities.
\end_layout

