\begin_layout Standard
The technical evolution of speed recommendation systems such as Intelligent Speed Assistance (ISA) has historically followed a trajectory from passive information retrieval to inform the driver about the current legal speed limit 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "ehrlichLAVIAFrenchISA2003,driscollLaviaEvaluationPotential2007"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 to active,
 more intelligent recommendations.
 Initially,
 research and industrial efforts were primarily focused on the robust identification of legal speed limits leveraging digital maps and accurate GPS positioning 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bahlmannMultimodalSpeedLimit2008,danielFusingNavigationVision2014,danielMultilevelDempsterShaferSpeed2012"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 The primary goal was informational,
 to ensure the driver is always aware of the current legal constraint.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As ISA is becoming manatory due to EU regulations 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "etsc2017isa,etsc2018isa,eu2019regulation,eu2021delegated"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 modern vehicles are no longer isolated agents but connected entities capable of gathering and processing a large amount of real-time data regarding traffic density,
 weather conditions or road surface quality,
 etc...
 This shift allows for a transition from static speed limit advisory to intelligent speed recommendation systems advising an optimal speed profile accounting for the dynamic complexity of the road context.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As the industrial scope of speed recommendation expands,
 the deployment of such intelligent systems in a commercial vehicle requires satisfying a rigorous set of requirements which are categorized in the remainder of this section.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Multi-Objective Optimization
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Unlike purely advisory ISA systems,
 intelligent speed recommendation acts as a high-level controller that must solve a multi-objective optimization problem.
 The recommendation must provide a clear added value to the stakeholders (e.g fleet managers or individual drivers).
 This requires the formulation of a cost function that negotiates between conflicting objectives.
 Here are some examples:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Energy Efficiency
\series default
:
 Minimizing fuel consumption for thermic vehicles or optimizing energy recovery through regenrative braking and discharge for eletric vehicles (EVs).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Temporal Efficiency
\series default
:
 Reducing travel time while maintaining a smooth speed profile.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Driving Comfort
\series default
:
 Avoiding aggressive longitudinal maneuvers that could degrade the passenger experience or increase the cognitive load of the driver.
\end_layout

