\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
For HRC the controller must be able to anticipate human behaviors to better synergise with him.
 In this thesis we chose to abstract the problem as a MPC,
 considering learning a predictive model of the vehicle driven by the driver.
 In this context,
 the actuator of the system is the human (shared controllability).
 So the goal is to influence the human decision as he is the one who influences the low level control and keeps the authority on his vehicle.
 In this thesis work the following assumption is made:
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Acceptance will be greater if the predictive model takes into account the driving behavior of the driver than if not.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 => we tackle driver acceptance by not considering the problem as an autonomous driving problem but by considering the driver in the loop,
 modeling his behavior.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
However,
 large variety of human drivers.
 Every profile is different.
 So there is a need to adapt to each individual driver.
 Solutions ?
 either (1) a large fundational model that would be able to encapsulate every type of driver profile to then predict their behavior (it would necessitate to find the profile of the driver beforehand),
 or (2) our solution with an online learning algorithm that continuously model the behavior of the driver => as the behavior can change,
 there is a need to adapt to non-stationarities
\end_layout

